          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Masterpieces and Writing Style</strong><br>\nDespite a brief literary career, Keats produced poems that remain significant in English literature, including \"Ode to a Nightingale,\" \"Ode on a Grecian Urn,\" and \"Ode to Melancholy.\" His writing is known for its sensual imagery and contemplative nature, focusing on the beauty of the ephemeral world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy and Inspirations</strong><br>\nKeats developed a philosophy he termed \"Negative Capability,\" the idea that a person could transcend context and uncertainties to be in the presence of beauty. This concept deeply influenced his work, reflecting his personal struggle with mortality and desire for transcendence.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Love and The Bright Star</strong><br>\nKeats fell deeply in love with Fanny Brawne in the latter part of his life. This relationship inspired some of his most passionate poetry and is evident in his letters to Brawne. He also wrote the sonnet \"Bright Star\" as a love letter to her, which has become one of his most famous pieces.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
