          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Illness and Creative Burst</strong><br>\nThe onset of tuberculosis, the disease that had killed his mother and brother, marked Keats's final years. During this period, he experienced a significant creative burst, producing some of his best poetry despite his deteriorating health and personal hardships.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Days in Rome</strong><br>\nKeats moved to Rome in 1820 in the hope that its warmer climate would improve his health. His final months were spent in the care of friends, including the painter Joseph Severn, who attended to him until his death in February 1821.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Posthumous Fame and Influence</strong><br>\nKeats died at the age of 25, largely unrecognized by the critics of his day. It was only after his death that his work gained the recognition it deserved, with his letters and poems now considered amongst the finest in English literature and a testament to the Romantic era's artistic and intellectual vigor.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
